# Hypervisor devices: virtio's NIC and disks; the NoCloud ISO, as a SCSI
# CD-ROM, for Lima; and the ACPI power button. Clouds' own devices are in
# prod.modules. And the root itself: erofs, on a loop device, through
# dm-verity, which stage0 mounts read-only in every form.
virtio_net virtio_blk sr_mod isofs evdev button erofs loop dm-verity
# arm64's power button, where a device tree wires it to a PL061 GPIO line
# (QEMU's virt, Apple's VZ): the controller, whose line power-button reads,
# since linux-virt has no gpio-keys driver to make it a key.
aarch64: gpio-pl061
# The victim's filesystem bite leaves behind: ext4 (Debian, Ubuntu, and
# werewolf's own disk, and /data's everywhere), xfs (Rocky), btrfs
# (Fedora). xfs and btrfs load only on a slot that is on them (`@FS:`;
# stage0 tells modload): btrfs's raid6 times itself for 0.17 s at load.
# btrfs asks the crypto API for its checksum at mount time, after the
# loader is closed, so crc32c (its default, and Fedora's) is loaded with
# it; xxhash64 and blake2b checksums would need xxhash_generic and
# blake2b_generic.
ext4
@xfs: xfs
@btrfs: btrfs crc32c-cryptoapi
# FAT, for werewolf's own disk (docs/design/native-boot.md): the updater and
# commit write the EFI partition. 437 and utf8 are FAT's default codepage
# and iocharset in Alpine's kernel.
vfat nls_cp437 nls_utf8
